## Why This Is Asked
Designing a versioning layer tests understanding of prompt drift, fallback logic, and observability in prompts.

## Key Concepts
- Versioned prompts, fallback strategies
- Signals: intent, confidence, version_stability
- Edge cases: outdated version, conflicting signals

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal version routing prototype
function routePrompt(prompt, version, scores) {
  const versions = ['v1','v2','v3'];
  let chosen = version && versions.includes(version) ? version : versions
    .map((v,i)=>({v, s: (scores && scores[i]) || 0}))
    .sort((a,b)=> b.s - a.s)
    [0].v;

  // Fallback logic for edge cases
  if (scores[chosen] < threshold || isOutdated(chosen)) {
    chosen = findMostStableVersion(versions, chosen);
    logFallback(chosen, prompt, scores);
  }

  return {
    version: chosen,
    plan: generatePlan(prompt, chosen),
    payload: buildPayload(prompt, chosen)
  };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Interacting across tenants requires real-time policy-compliant routing. The candidate must design a routing/policy layer that respects ABAC, handles sensitive content, and balances safety with latency, all while exposing auditable decisions.

## Key Concepts

- Prompt intent extraction and sensitive-term detection
- ABAC policy evaluation against user attributes
- Model variant selection under policy constraints
- Auditing: fingerprinting, latency, policy_score, route trace
- Lightweight prototype with mock models for realism

## Code Example

```javascript
function routePrompt(prompt, tenant) {
  const intents = /summarize|export|analyze|decrypt/.test(prompt) ? 'specialized' : 'general';
  const sensitive = /PII|password|secret/.test(prompt);
  const policy = (tenant.policies || []).find(p => p.action === 'route');
  const model = policy && policy.allows && !sensitive ? 'fast-safe' : 'strict';
  const fingerprint = hash(prompt + tenant.id);
  return { finalPrompt: prompt, model, fingerprint };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle policy updates and cache invalidation?
- What metrics would you collect to detect model drift after routing decisions?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to extend routing with governance constraints and to design testable prototypes.

## Key Concepts
- multi-module routing with governance
- signal design: intent, data_sensitivity, governance_policy
- edge-case handling: conflicts, missing role

## Code Example
```javascript
function route(prompt, signals){
  if (signals.governance_policy === 'restrict') return {module:'Data-Governance', payload:{prompt}};
  if (signals.data_sensitivity === 'PII') return {module:'PII-redactor', payload:{prompt}};
  return {module:'SQL-builder', payload:{prompt}};
}
module.exports = route;
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you unit test the routing logic for edge cases like conflicting signals?
- How would you extend the router to support additional governance policies without breaking existing behavior?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses ability to design a tiny, deterministic routing loop for an offline-first finance tool, with explicit module boundaries and safety signals.

## Key Concepts
- Prompt routing signals: intent, data_privacy, offline_capable
- Edge-case handling: no_cache, conflicting signals
- Module boundaries: TransactionFilter, SummaryComposer, LocalCacheManager
- Deterministic payload construction

## Code Example
```javascript
// Tiny router example (pseudo)
function routePrompt(prompt, offline) {
  const signals = extractSignals(prompt);
  const module = offline ? 'LocalCacheManager' : 'NetworkFetch';
  return {module, signals};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test cache-miss scenarios and signal conflicts?
- How to extend to multi-account data and multiple currencies?

## Why This Is Asked
The question probes practical prompt governance: how to prove provenance and detect tampering from model transformations in a multi-tenant setting. It tests attack awareness, deterministic auditing, and safe-guard design under real-world noise.

## Key Concepts
- Watermarking robustness under paraphrase/translation
- Non-semantic embedding and tamper detection
- Audit logging for per-tenant prompts
- Edge-case handling and false positives

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal watermark prototype (JS)
function addWatermark(prompt, tag) {
  return prompt + " [WM:" + tag + "]";
}
function detectWatermark(text) {
  const m = text.match(/\[WM:([A-Za-z0-9._-]+)\]/);
  return m ? m[1] : null;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you evaluate watermark robustness across languages/models?
- How would you scale to thousands of tenants with dynamic watermark policies?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a scalable, secure prompt pipeline that enforces per-tenant policies, blocks prompt-injection, and records privacy-preserving audits in real time.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant policy schemas (allowed_actions, data_classifications)
- Safety shims against system-prompt leakage and prompt-injection
- Privacy-preserving reasoning traces in audit logs
- Latency budgeting and streaming guarantees

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal prototype components for the three stages
function validatePolicy(prompt, tenantPolicy) {
  // check allowed_actions and data_classifications
  const okAction = tenantPolicy.allowed_actions.includes('execute') || prompt.includes('export');
  const safeData = !/SECRET|PASSWORD/i.test(prompt);
  return okAction && safeData;
}

function guardPrompt(prompt, policy) {
  // simple sanitizer to remove system prompts or injected cues
  let p = prompt.replace(/system prompt:.*$/i, '').trim();
  return p;
}

function logAudit(input, modelVersion, latency, reasoning) {
  return {
    input: input, modelVersion, latency, reasoningTrace: reasoning
  };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test this against non-determinism and ensure consistent audits at scale?
- What metrics would you collect to detect policy drift or leakage risk over time?
